flow "Vector clocks"
summary "Tracks per-node logical counters so replicas can tell when one version causally supersedes another versus when concurrent writes conflict."
tags clocks causality consistency
level advanced
source "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vector_clock"
related distributed-systems/lamport-clocks distributed-systems/crdt-counter
layout algorithm=stress edges=straight

group cluster "Replicas"
node a "Node A" service in=cluster tone=primary
node b "Node B" service in=cluster
node c "Node C" service in=cluster
edge a > b noarrow
edge b > c noarrow
edge a > c noarrow
array vc "Vector clocks [A,B,C]" keys=a,b,c = [0,0,0] [0,0,0] [0,0,0]

scenario causal "Causal ordering proves no conflict"
  step a-write "Node A writes x=1"
    "Each node maintains a vector of counters — one slot per node. Before recording `x=1`, Node A increments its own slot to `[1,0,0]`."
    state a active
    label vc#a "[1,0,0]"
    state vc#a active with
  step a-to-b "Node A replicates x=1 to Node B"
    "Node A sends `x=1` tagged with `[1,0,0]`. On receipt, Node B merges element-wise `max([0,0,0], [1,0,0])` and increments its own slot to `[1,1,0]`."
    send a > b "x=1 [1,0,0]"
    label vc#b "[1,1,0]"
    state b,vc#b active with
  step b-update "Node B updates x=2"
    "Node B writes `x=2` and increments its slot to `[1,2,0]`. Because every entry in `[1,0,0]` is `<=` `[1,2,0]` (and strictly smaller in slot B), `x=2` **causally descends** from `x=1`."
    detail "Formally, `U < V` if and only if `U[i] <= V[i]` for every slot `i` and `U[j] < V[j]` for at least one `j`. Unlike scalar Lamport clocks (where `L(a) < L(b)` does not guarantee causality), vector clock comparison is an exact isomorphism of the causal partial order."
    label vc#b "[1,2,0]"
    send b > c "x=2 [1,2,0]"
    label vc#c "[1,2,1]"
    state c,vc#c success with
    callout c "[1,0,0] < [1,2,0]: safe overwrite"

scenario concurrent "Concurrent writes detected as conflict" from=causal/a-write
  step b-independent "Node B writes x=9 without seeing A"
    "Before A's message arrives, Node B independently accepts `x=9` and increments its own slot to `[0,1,0]`."
    state b warning
    label vc#b "[0,1,0]"
    state vc#b warning with
  step both-arrive "Node C receives both versions"
    "Node C receives `x=1 [1,0,0]` from A and `x=9 [0,1,0]` from B. Now C compares the two vectors slot by slot."
    send a > c "x=1 [1,0,0]"
    send b > c "x=9 [0,1,0]" with
    state c error
    effect c shake
  step conflict "Neither vector dominates: true conflict"
    "In slot A, `1 > 0`; in slot B, `0 < 1`. Because neither clock dominates the other, the writes are **concurrent** — C keeps both siblings for application merge instead of silently losing a write."
    detail "Leaderless stores such as Amazon Dynamo and Riak use version vectors to surface concurrent **siblings** to the application (for example, merging shopping-cart items). The trade-off is `O(N)` metadata growth with the number of distinct writers."
    label vc#c "[1,1,1] CONFLICT"
    state vc#c error with
    callout c "[1,0,0] || [0,1,0] -> keep siblings"
